{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The oath of HaShem etc.</b> Rashi explained that if he laid hands on it and afterward it was taken by force, he is liable for forced losses. And elsewhere he explained: even if he returned it afterward, since he laid hands on it once, he is liable for its forced loss forever. And this is puzzling to R. Tam, for in the passage above it is written ״או נשבר או נשבה אין רואה״ — this implies that if there are witnesses that it broke or was captured, he brings proof and is exempt without an oath. And according to Rashi's words, what did proof avail him? In any case he must swear, lest he have laid hands on it and returned it to its place, for he is liable even if there are witnesses who saw that it was forcibly taken from its original place — and the witnesses cannot know whether he laid hands on it and returned it to its original place. Therefore R. Tam said: even if he laid hands on it, since he returned it to its place before it was taken by force, he is exempt."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:54.195899719Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}